## Research Use and Validation

The software provides computational tools for molecular structure and graph
construction, preparation, modification, and analysis, including protein-related
workflows when provided. It is supplied as a research tool, without any
representation that its algorithms or outputs have been validated for a
particular small molecule, protein, molecular complex, chemical transformation,
biological target, assay, or downstream application.

You are responsible for determining whether the software is appropriate for
your intended use and for independently checking the scientific correctness
and chemical validity of its inputs, assumptions, transformations, and outputs.
For protein-related workflows, this includes checking structural preparation,
atom and residue identities, biological assembly selection, and relevant
protonation, missing-structure, cofactor, and binding-site assumptions to the
extent applicable to the intended use.
Before relying on results for synthesis, experimental work, drug discovery,
drug development, or manufacturing, you must arrange verification and
validation appropriate to that use, qualified professional review, and
compliance with applicable laws and institutional requirements.

A computed structure, score, functional-group assignment, or molecular
transformation does not by itself establish synthetic feasibility, safety,
toxicity, biological activity, protein function or stability, binding affinity,
efficacy, or manufacturability. Permission to
use the software, including commercial permission, does not constitute the
licensor's validation, recommendation, or approval of a downstream experiment,
compound, product, or application.

Maintenance, technical support, correction of errors, and future releases are
not promised. These conditions allocate responsibilities and do not certify
results or substitute for appropriate experimental validation and safeguards.

